  description="Work Joe Fazio has built: a time-banking system, a security risk assessment, and this site."
>
  <h1>Projects</h1>

  <p>
    Four projects. Three came out of my final year of coursework at James Madison
    University, and the fourth is this site. The employers, dates and courses
    behind them are on my <a href="/resume.pdf">resume</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>ACC Timebank</h2>
  <p class="meta">Spring 2026 · lead developer · Google Apps Script, Google Sheets, Python</p>

  <p>
    A time-banking system for the Alachua Community Collective, a mutual-aid
    group in Florida whose members trade hours of work instead of money. ACC
    asked for two tracks: a fast, low-cost version built on tools they already
    had, and a fuller custom system. I was lead developer on the first and did
    most of the requirements gathering across the whole engagement. It appears on
    my resume as the Quick Hit Timebank System.
  </p>

  <p>What I built:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Transaction logic for crediting and debiting hours between members</li>
    <li>Admin reporting</li>
    <li>
      A burnout-risk flag that surfaces members who are hoarding hours or giving
      away far more than they take, so an admin knows to check in on them
    </li>
    <li>A Python script to migrate existing member records into the new system</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Two decisions shaped the build. I started in AppSheet and moved off it
    because it could not express the transaction logic ACC needed. I rebuilt on
    Google Apps Script with a Google Sheet as the database, because ACC's member
    intake already ran through a Google Form: same ecosystem, no migration, and
    no new tool for the people already using it.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Status:</strong> the capstone ended and ACC no longer runs the
    system. The deployment sits behind a Google login and there is no public
    repository, so there is no live demo to link. A fuller writeup with code
    excerpts is the next thing I am adding here.
  </p>

  <h2>BRHS Cybersecurity Risk Assessment</h2>
  <p class="meta">Spring 2026 · co-author · NIST CSF 2.0, CIS Controls v8</p>

  <p>
    A consulting report for a fictional healthcare organization: identify the
    weaknesses, rank them, and recommend hardening a real budget could absorb.
    We worked against NIST's Cybersecurity Framework and CIS Controls v8 rather
    than inventing our own categories, so a reader can check every finding
    against a published standard. This was coursework and the organization was
    invented for the assignment. The method is the part that carries over.
  </p>

  <h2>Capsim Business Simulation</h2>
  <p class="meta">Spring 2026 · strategic management</p>

  <p>
    A multi-round simulation in which a team runs a company against competitors.
    We ran a niche differentiation strategy aimed at the high-tech segments,
    making pricing, R&amp;D, marketing and production decisions each round and
    carrying the results into the next. It is the closest thing in my coursework
    to deciding with incomplete information and a cost attached to being wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>This site</h2>
  <p class="meta">2026 · ongoing · Astro, Cloudflare Pages, GitHub Actions</p>

  <p>
    A static site built with Astro and hosted on Cloudflare Pages, with DNS and
    TLS I manage myself. Every push runs a build in GitHub Actions, and a branch
    rule blocks anything from reaching the main branch unless that build passes.
    The repository is public and includes a decision log with a written reason
    for every choice in it, including the ones I reversed. Terraform for the
    Cloudflare configuration and a small Cloudflare Worker are the next pieces.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/joefazio1/joefazio-portfolio">Source on GitHub</a>
  </p>
